\caption(The original four-pair zero-cost witness is retained. Twelve positive cells and their selected path come from the same recurrence that returns the result.)
\alttext(A native three-by-four grid of prefix costs highlights a four-cell alignment through repeated ones, twos and threes.)

> Derive the recurrence and its witness

A legal path starts at (0,0), ends at (n−1,m−1), and advances by
(1,0), (0,1) or (1,1). The first coordinate indexes a and the second
indexes b. Both endpoints are mandatory. Its unnormalized loss is the
sum of |a_i−b_j| over all visited pairs. Thus lengths may differ, and
one observation may be paired with several observations of the other input.

Let D(i,j) denote the minimum cost for prefixes of lengths i and j.
Set D(0,0)=0 and the other empty-prefix boundaries to infinity. Then

\[
D(i,j)=|a_{i-1}-b_{j-1}|+
\min\{D(i-1,j-1),D(i-1,j),D(i,j-1)\}.
\]

Every legal final step has one of those predecessors. Removing it leaves
a legal prefix path; appending it to a minimizing prefix path attains the
recurrence. Induction in increasing i+j therefore proves optimality.
Traceback follows a minimizing predecessor until the origin. Stored ties
prefer diagonal, then advancing a, then advancing b, preserving the
original API's deterministic witness. Each step advances at least one
axis, so max(n,m)<=L<=n+m−1 for L returned pairs.

This implementation uses scalar absolute loss, with no window, slope
constraint, step weighting, square root or length normalization. It is
an unrestricted variant of the time-alignment framework. The cited
Sakoe–Chiba paper also studies weighting, normalization and constraints;
those additional rules are not silently included here.

> A useful similarity is not necessarily a metric

Unequal sequences [0] and [0,0] have zero DTW cost. The triangle
inequality can fail too: take A=[0,0,0,0], B=[0,1] and C=[1,1,1,1].
A can repeat its zeros against B's first zero and pay only once for
B's one. B can similarly pay only once for its zero against C.
Their two costs are one each, while every pair between A and C costs
one and any legal path has at least four pairs. Hence d(A,C)=4>1+1.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dtw_nonmetric.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<double>a{0,0,0,0},b{0,1},c{1,1,1,1};
  const auto ab=dm::geometry::dynamic_time_warping(a,b),bc=dm::geometry::dynamic_time_warping(b,c),ac=dm::geometry::dynamic_time_warping(a,c);
  const auto repeated=dm::geometry::dynamic_time_warping({0},{0,0});
  std::print("Unequal stretched zeros: distance {}. Triangle: d(A,C)={} > d(A,B)+d(B,C)={}+{}={}.\n",repeated.distance,ac.distance,ab.distance,bc.distance,ab.distance+bc.distance);
  if(!(ac.distance>ab.distance+bc.distance))throw std::runtime_error("triangle control failed");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(64,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=3.2;box.height=1.3;box.font_size=24;
  d.node("a",{0,0},"A\n[0,0,0,0]",box).node("b",{4,3},"B\n[0,1]",box).node("c",{8,0},"C\n[1,1,1,1]",box)
   .edge("a","b",std::format("{:.0f}",ab.distance)).edge("b","c",std::format("{:.0f}",bc.distance))
   .edge("a","c",std::format("{:.0f} > {:.0f}+{:.0f}",ac.distance,ab.distance,bc.distance))
   .text({4,4.3},"Unrestricted DTW violates the triangle inequality",24).save_svg("build/dtw_nonmetric.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Unequal stretched zeros: distance 0. Triangle: d(A,C)=4 > d(A,B)+d(B,C)=1+1=2.
\image(figures/dtw_nonmetric.svg)
// result_end
\caption(These are computed DTW costs, not arbitrary edge weights. Elastic alignment loses both separation of unequal sequences and the triangle inequality.)
\alttext(A native triangle connects sequences A, B and C with computed costs one, one and four, showing four greater than one plus one.)

For equal lengths the diagonal path is always feasible, so DTW is at
most the rigid L1 sum over matching sample indices. The earlier report
called its comparator Euclidean even though it summed absolute
errors. We use the precise name rigid L1 throughout; a squared-loss
or Euclidean DTW variant would define a different objective.

> Retain the original thirty-sample sinusoid

Sample a(u)=sin(2πu) and b(u)=sin(2πu^1.6) at u=i/29.
The same waveform has a nonlinear timing distortion. The rigid L1
comparator uses the diagonal witness; the optimal path can repeat
indices to align crests and troughs. Vertical offsets in the figure
only separate the displayed curves and do not enter either cost.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dtw_sine.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numbers>
#include <numeric>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto u=dm::seql(0,29,30)/29;
  const std::vector<double>a=dm::sin(2*std::numbers::pi*u),b=dm::sin(2*std::numbers::pi*dm::pow(u,1.6));
  const auto t=dm::geometry::dynamic_time_warping_trace(a,b);
  std::vector<int> diagonal(a.size());std::iota(diagonal.begin(),diagonal.end(),0);
  const auto rigid=dm::geometry::dtw_path_cost(a,b,diagonal,diagonal);
  std::print("Original sinusoid: DTW {:.5f}; rigid L1 {:.4f}; path pairs {}; cells {}; capture entries {}.\n",t.result.distance,rigid,t.result.path_a.size(),t.work.cells,t.captured_entries);
  std::print("Path witness recomputed cost {:.17g}.\n",dm::geometry::dtw_path_cost(a,b,t.result.path_a,t.result.path_b));
  dm::geometry::plot_dtw_alignment(a,b,t.result).title("Original sinusoid: timing flexibility lowers absolute loss")
   .y_limits(-3,4.2).save_svg("build/dtw_sine.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Original sinusoid: DTW 1.41447; rigid L1 12.2668; path pairs 35; cells 900; capture entries 1860.
Path witness recomputed cost 1.4144703472582707.
\image(figures/dtw_sine.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original DTW distance, rigid L1 comparison and 35-pair witness are recomputed. Gray correspondence segments use the actual optimal path; displayed offsets do not change the data being compared.)
\alttext(Blue and red offset sinusoidal curves are joined by actual gray DTW correspondences, which shift with the nonlinear timing distortion.)

> Align recorded motions through the canonical data API

GunPoint contains two actors performing gun-draw and pointing actions.
The released univariate signals track the right hand's x-coordinate.
The official archive has 50 training and 150 test records, each with
150 samples. The existing canonical preparation holds ten original
training records out for validation, leaving 40 fitting records; the
150 official test records remain separate.

We use fixed fitting records: the first stored record, the first later
record with the same label, and the first with the other label. Selection
uses metadata and storage order, not the distances we are about to show.
The canonical loader retains original source ids and raw Float32 ingress
values, widened here for scalar DTW. No additional normalization or
synthetic signal is introduced. These two illustrative alignments do
not estimate classification accuracy or establish actor independence.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dtw_gunpoint.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/time_series_data.hpp>
#include <numeric>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto base=dm::datasets::prepare_example_data("signals",dm::datasets::data_home());
  const auto split=dm::nn::models::LoadSeries(std::filesystem::path(base)/"time_series");
  const auto& fit=split.pools[0];const auto labels=fit.labels.to_vector<int>(),ids=fit.source_ids.to_vector<int>();
  const auto a=dm::nn::select(fit.signals,0,0).reshape({150}).to_vector<double>();
  std::size_t same=labels.size(),different=labels.size();
  for(std::size_t i=1;i<labels.size();++i){if(labels[i]==labels[0]&&same==labels.size())same=i;if(labels[i]!=labels[0]&&different==labels.size())different=i;}
  if(same==labels.size()||different==labels.size())throw std::runtime_error("missing source classes");
  std::print("Canonical pools: {} fitting, {} validation, {} official test records. Reference source id {}, class {} (0=gun, 1=point).\n\n",fit.size(),split.pools[1].size(),split.pools[2].size(),ids[0],labels[0]);
  std::print("| pair | other source id | other class | DTW | rigid L1 | path pairs | DP cells |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);std::vector<int> diagonal(150);std::iota(diagonal.begin(),diagonal.end(),0);
  for(auto row:{same,different}){
    const auto b=dm::nn::select(fit.signals,0,row).reshape({150}).to_vector<double>();
    const auto t=dm::geometry::dynamic_time_warping_trace(a,b);const auto name=row==same?"same class":"other class";
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {} | {} |\n",name,ids[row],labels[row],t.result.distance,dm::geometry::dtw_path_cost(a,b,diagonal,diagonal),t.result.path_a.size(),t.work.cells);
    auto plot=dm::geometry::plot_dtw_alignment(a,b,t.result,4);
    plot.title(std::format("{}: source {} to {}",name,ids[0],ids[row])).y_limits(-8,10);layout.add(plot);
  }
  layout.size(1100,600).title("Fixed original GunPoint pairs: alignment evidence, not a benchmark score")
   .fit_panels().save_svg("build/dtw_gunpoint.svg");
}
```
